[38;2;124;124;124m\frac{e^{ix}}{x} dx$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a classic integral. Let me think about it carefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe integrand $\frac{e^{ix}}{x}$ has a singularity at $x=0$, so we need to [0m
[38;2;124;124;124minterpret this as a principal value integral.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me compute $\text{P.V.} \int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} \frac{e^{ix}}{x} dx$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe have $e^{ix} = \cos x + i \sin x$, so[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$\frac{e^{ix}}{x} = \frac{\cos x}{x} + i \frac{\sin x}{x}$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe real part: $\frac{\cos x}{x}$ is an odd function, so its principal value [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mintegral over $(-\infty, +\infty)$ is $0$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe imaginary part: $\frac{\sin x}{x}$ is an even function, and $\int_{-\infty}[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m^{+\infty} \frac{\sin x}{x} dx = \pi$ (the well-known Dirichlet integral).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo $\text{P.V.} \int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} \frac{e^{ix}}{x} dx = 0 + i\pi = i\pi$.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mLet me verify this with contour integration to be rigorous.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mConsider $f(z) = \frac{e^{iz}}{z}$. We use a contour that goes along the real [0m
[38;2;124;124;124maxis from $-R$ to $-\varepsilon$, then a small semicircle $C_\varepsilon$ of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mradius $\varepsilon$ in the upper half-plane from $-\varepsilon$ to $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\varepsilon$, then along the real axis from $\varepsilon$ to $R$, then a large [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msemicircle $C_R$ of radius $R$ in the upper half-plane from $R$ to $-R$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe function $f(z) = e^{iz}/z$ has a simple pole at $z=0$ with residue $1$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBy the residue theorem, the integral over the closed contour (which encloses no [0m[38;2;124;124;124msingularity since the pole is on the contour, excluded by the small semicircle) [0m[38;2;124;124;124mis... wait, actually the pole at $z=0$ is excluded by the indentation. So the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcontour encloses no singularities, and the integral is $0$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$\int_{-R}^{-\varepsilon} \frac{e^{ix}}{x}dx + \int_{C_\varepsilon} [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\frac{e^{iz}}{z}dz + \int_{\varepsilon}^{R}\frac{e^{ix}}{x}dx + [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\int_{C_R}\frac{e^{iz}}{z}dz = 0$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAs $R \to \infty$, $\int_{C_R} \frac{e^{iz}}{z}dz \to 0$ by Jordan's lemma [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(since $e^{iz}$ decays in the upper half plane).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor the small semicircle $C_\varepsilon$: parametrize $z = \varepsilon [0m
[38;2;124;124;124me^{i\theta}$, $\theta$ from $\pi$ to $0$ (going from $-\varepsilon$ to $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\varepsilon$ in the upper half plane). Then $dz = i\varepsilon e^{i\theta} [0m
[38;2;124;124;124md\theta$ and $z = \varepsilon e^{i\theta}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$$\int_{C_\varepsilon} \frac{e^{iz}}{z}dz = \int_{\pi}^{0} \frac{e^{i\varepsilon[0m[38;2;124;124;124me^{i\theta}}}{\varepsilon e^{i\theta}} \cdot i\varepsilon e^{i\theta} d\theta = [0m[38;2;124;124;124mi\int_{\pi}^{0} e^{i\varepsilon e^{i\theta}} d\theta$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAs $\varepsilon \to 0$, $e^{i\varepsilon e^{i\theta}} \to 1$, so this approaches[0m[38;2;124;124;124m$i\int_{\pi}^{0} d\theta = i(0 - \pi) = -i\pi$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo taking limits:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$\text{P.V.}\int_{-\infty}^{+\infty}\frac{e^{ix}}{x}dx + (-i\pi) + 0 = 0$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$$\text{P.V.}\int_{-\infty}^{+\infty}\frac{e^{ix}}{x}dx = i\pi$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis confirms the answer is $i\pi$.[0m

